    "sources:Content_en.txt": {
      "fingerprint": "1f216dac325fbe1eb9b9dd8a0f582d7acd192e74cd513f317d9ba2b01e82ed86",
      "kind": "sources",
      "status": "needs_reference",
      "issues": [
        "Fetch official supporting evidence for: Example: “Which year after 2024 will have the same calendar as 2024?” 2024 is a leap year, so 2024 + 28 = 2052.",
        "Fetch official supporting evidence for: 🧠 If 2025 is ordinary, will 2031 have the same calendar? Yes: odd days from 2025 to 2030 add up to 1+1+1+2+1+1 = 7 (a multiple of 7), and both 2025 and 2031 are ordinary years. When in doubt, always count odd days instead of blindly adding 6 or 11.",
        "Fetch official supporting evidence for: “Which year after 2004 will have the same calendar as 2004?” Blindly adding 6 or 11 fails here: 2004 is a leap year, so it can only match another leap year whose odd-day total in between is a multiple of 7 — that happens after 28 years (2032). Options like 2010, 2011, 2015 trap students who memorised a rigid “+6 or +11” pattern without checking whether the year is leap. Always check the leap/ordinary nature first, then count odd days."
      ],
